    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what do you mean about goblins? i was just asking about my plant. can you just explain the actual biological reason for the mushy leaves without the weird comparisons?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the excessive goblin references in my previous response; I clearly got carried away with the metaphorical train and forgot to keep it grounded in your actual question.\n\nThe mushy, yellowing lower leaves on your Monstera are almost certainly a sign of **root rot**, which happens when the plant is overwatered. When you water the soil too frequently, the excess moisture sits around the roots instead of draining away, creating a breeding ground for fungi and bacteria. These pathogens attack the root system, causing the roots to die and turn brown or black.\n\nBecause the roots are responsible for absorbing water and nutrients, once they are damaged, the plant can no longer support the leaves above ground. This causes the older, lower leaves to lose turgor pressure, turning yellow and becoming soft or mushy as the plant shuts them down to conserve energy. To fix this, you need to let the soil dry out completely before watering again, and you may need to repot the plant into fresh, well-draining soil to give the roots a chance to heal.",
